{"name":"Green Peridot","hex":"#a7c668","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"peridot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A transparent olive green form of olivine, used as a gem.","A yellow-green color, like that of the peridot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peridot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peridot","blocks":[{"text":"Peridot, sometimes called chrysolite, is a yellow-green transparent variety of olivine, specifically the magnesium rich end member called forsterite. Peridot is one of the few gemstones that occur in only one color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg/330px-Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peridot","wikidata":"Q288732"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png/250px-Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png/250px-Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The atomic scale structure of olivine looking along the a axis. Oxygen is shown in red, silicon in pink, and magnesium/iron in blue. A projection of the unit cell is shown by the black rectangle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/500px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/1280px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/500px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pallasite meteorite with olive-green peridot c